---
/**
 * t39 · Skin
 *
 * The outline reference, filled in.
 *
 * An outlined bubble is a drawing of a bubble. This is the same universal
 * oval — no clipped shoulder, no platform gesture, nothing anyone has to
 * learn — but built as an object, so it can sit beside Plozz's TV and Mozz's
 * disc without being the one flat thing on the shelf.
 *
 * The depth is taken from what the object is. A speech bubble is a skin with
 * air behind it, so the tone is a membrane reading: one light from the upper
 * left, wrapped round the silhouette and falling off inward at a fixed rate.
 * That puts the whole tone spend in the outer band and leaves the middle a
 * single violet — the family's own rule, arrived at the way the siblings
 * arrive at it, from the physics of the container rather than from a
 * gradient.
 *
 * The tail is not applied. The body and the tail are one field, blended
 * before anything is rasterised, so the lower-left arc keeps going and
 * narrows to two pixels. There is no seam to look at because there is no
 * seam. It is shaded by the same wrapped light as the body, which is what
 * makes it read as part of the same skin rather than a wedge in the same
 * colour.
 *
 * Ten violets. The ramp is cut in two on purpose: the six dark steps all
 * clear 4.5:1 against the white face, and the light steps above them are
 * reserved for rim and highlight, where the face never reaches. The middle
 * measures 5.15:1.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

<MarkFrame size={size} title="Strozz — Skin">
